The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (unlikely(copied < len && !PageUptodate(page))) {
    copied = 0;
    goto out;
```
Reason:
This condition checks if the number of bytes copied is less than the expected length and if the page is not marked as up-to-date. If both conditions are true, it sets `copied` to 0 and exits the function without marking the page as up-to-date, even when the write operation was successful.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (!PageUptodate(page)) {
    if (unlikely(copied < len)) {
        copied = 0;
        goto out;
    } else if (len == PAGE_SIZE) {
        SetPageUptodate(page);
    }
}
```
Reason:
The fix modifies the logic to first check if the page is not up-to-date. If the copied bytes are less than the length, it still sets `copied` to 0, but if the length equals `PAGE_SIZE`, it explicitly marks the page as up-to-date. This ensures that the page is correctly updated when the write operation is successful, addressing the issue introduced by the previous commit.

